ite to the  publish openly phase of this process at  west liberty but we're hoping to get  there  someday soon  we also because we have this foundation  in undergraduate research are able to  incorporate more bioinformatic analysis  into the students research so they have  generated their data  now they can sit down and analyze it  rather than just handing it off to  someone  and then getting back the the finished  figures  so we're trying to work on getting them  to  figure out how to actually analyze their  data a little bit with us  and just in general emphasize that this  is problem solving you don't have to  have  all this code memorized that's not  really how bioinformatics happens but  you need to know where to find the tools  and how to use them and be able to  think your way through these problems  that come up  so like i said i've done some k-12  recruitment of course with covid it's  been kind of limited so this is very  recent development  actually last weekend i did a small  recruitment event where i used these  ozobots  which you can these are little  programmable robots that follow color  codes  so you can teach young kids how to  program in a really  intuitive sort of way so i had grades  three through five  young girls working on programming their  ozobots to  solve a problem or find its way through  a maze  and then  for the older kids i have actually gone  into the high school classrooms and i've  talked about next generation sequencing  introduced the concept of bioinformatics  and then we've done some really basic  mapping and variant calling on a very  small scale so just to kind of get their  feet wet get them thinking about when  they go to college you know this could  be an option for them  kind of  adding on to that coming from that  we want them to have an option once they  get to college so we are developing a  bioinformatics major at our institution  um i am developing it so right now it's  just me um and i am i'm teaching a few  different courses that are going to  ultimately be part of this major so the  first one is biological programming  which sounds a little scary got to work  on that name i think a little bit  to make it more palatable to biology  students but this is just really basic  computing skills so you get introduced  to linux the command line  a lot of you know text based  bioinformatic file processing so lots of  fast queue fasta  file processing  just learning how to automate tasks  learning the basics of programming that  they probably haven't been exposed to at  all before so  it's a class for complete beginners and  it's kind of amazing how much progress  they can make in just a single semester  i will also ultimately be teaching what  we're going to call bioinformatics one  and two um right now i'm teaching  bioinformatics one and we are doing that  um  through uh the galaxy interface so this  is a completely online  server-based  interactive  bioinformatic analysis workflows  and i find that this is really  approachable for biology students who  want to use bioinformatic pipelines for  their research  but they are not so sure that they want  to go down the route of linux and  command line and super computing they  they just really have these small  projects that they're working on they  want to know  how do i run an rna-seq pipeline really  quickly without installing anything on  my computer or finding access to some  kind of  remote server so it's pretty accessible  and we've been doing some different  tutorials with them  to kind of go through some really basic  bioinformatic methods  and then we have a ton of affiliated  courses or what i'm terming affiliated  courses so things like phylogenetic  theory which i teach in the fall  applied microbiology where the students  actually get to sequence  microbiomes and then they will analyze  it with my help  genetics courses we're working more  bioinformatics more computing into the  basic core genetics that we teach at  west liberty  and then we have the opportunity for  these independent student research  projects as well  we're also  elsewhere on campus they are developing  a data science program um that's through  the the math department so i expect  there will be a lot of overlap between  those two majors  so  we're going to enhance research  experiences by allowing them to actually  generate their own next generation  sequencing data we have an oxford  nanopore minion and we have just secured  an iseq so we will have alumina short  read and long read data for them to  analyze  so it's a really exciting time for the  bio department  they're going to have  so much data and they're definitely  going to need to know how to analyze  this for their class projects  um so that includes everything from  amplicon sequencing  some people interested in doing edna  we've had a tick microbiome  project done i've used it for  conservation genomics so looking at  relatedness of  organisms of conservation concern  and then of course we can do  rna-seq as well  we also offer summer training  opportunities um we have the shore  program summer undergraduate research  experience  that's an ongoing program at west  liberty and i'm hoping to join in more  with that in the future and actually  training one-on-one students over the  summer to to do bioinformatic research  and then really specific to  bioinformatics we offer the  bioinformatics boot camp every summer  i say every summer last year was our  first year offering it and this year  will be our second  but this is a three-week event um  it requires no previous experience we go  from  having no computational experience to  doing basic bioinformatic pipelines by  the end of this project  so  we start with an introduction to linux  and the command line  then we go in the next couple of weeks  into specific pipelines so we give them  a script that has the pipeline and then  we break this pipeline down into  digestible chunks for them so that by  the end 