ite to the  publish openly phase of this process at  west liberty but we're hoping to get  there  someday soon  we also because we have this foundation  in undergraduate research are able to  incorporate more bioinformatic analysis  into the students research so they have  generated their data  now they can sit down and analyze it  rather than just handing it off to  someone  and then getting back the the finished  figures  so we're trying to work on getting them  to  figure out how to actually analyze their  data a little bit with us  and just in general emphasize that this  is problem solving you don't have to  have  all this code memorized that's not  really how bioinformatics happens but  you need to know where to find the tools  and how to use them and be able to  think your way through these problems  that come up  so like i said i've done some k-12  recruitment of course with covid it's  been kind of limited so this is very  recent development  actually last weekend i did a small  recruitment event where i used these  ozobots  which you can these are little  programmable robots that follow color  codes  so you can teach young kids how to  program in a really  intuitive sort of way so i had grades  three through five  young girls working on programming their  ozobots to  solve a problem or find its way through  a maze  and then  for the older kids i have actually gone  into the high school classrooms and i've  talked about next generation sequencing  introduced the concept of bioinformatics  and then we've done some really basic  mapping and variant calling on a very  small scale so just to kind of get their  feet wet get them thinking about when  they go to college you know this could  be an option for them  kind of  adding on to that coming from that  we want them to have an option once they  get to college so we are developing a  bioinformatics major at our institution  um i am developing it so right now it's  just me um and i am i'm teaching a few  different courses that are going to  ultimately be part of this major so the  first one is biological programming  which sounds a little scary got to work  on that name i think a little bit  to make it more palatable to biology  students but this is just really basic  computing skills so you get introduced  to linux the command line  a lot of you know text based  bioinformatic file processing so lots of  fast queue fasta  file processing  just learning how to automate tasks  learning the basics of programming that  they probably haven't been exposed to at  all before so  it's a class for complete beginners and  it's kind of amazing how much progress  they can make in just a single semester  i will also ultimately be teaching what  we're going to call bioinformatics one  and two um right now i'm teaching  bioinformatics one and we are doing that  um  through uh the galaxy interface so this  is a completely online  server-based  interactive  bioinformatic analysis workflows  and i find that this is really  approachable for biology students who  want to use bioinformatic pipelines for  their research  but they are not so sure that they want  to go down the route of linux and  command line and super computing they  they just really have these small  projects that they're working on they  want to know  how do i run an rna-seq pipeline really  quickly without installing anything on  my computer or finding access to some  kind of  remote server so it's pretty accessible  and we've been doing some different  tutorials with them  to kind of go through some really basic  bioinformatic methods  and then we have a ton of affiliated  courses or what i'm terming affiliated  courses so things like phylogenetic  theory which i teach in the fall  applied microbiology where the students  actually get to sequence  microbiomes and then they will analyze  it with my help  genetics courses we're working more  bioinformatics more computing into the  basic core genetics that we teach at  west liberty  and then we have the opportunity for  these independent student research  projects as well  we're also  elsewhere on campus they are developing  a data science program um that's through  the the math department so i expect  there will be a lot of overlap between  those two majors  so  we're going to enhance research  experiences by allowing them to actually  generate their own next generation  sequencing data we have an oxford  nanopore minion and we have just secured  an iseq so we will have alumina short  read and long read data for them to  analyze  so it's a really exciting time for the  bio department  they're going to have  so much data and they're definitely  going to need to know how to analyze  this for their class projects  um so that includes everything from  amplicon sequencing  some people interested in doing edna  we've had a tick microbiome  project done i've used it for  conservation genomics so looking at  relatedness of  organisms of conservation concern  and then of course we can do  rna-seq as well  we also offer summer training  opportunities um we have the shore  program summer undergraduate research  experience  that's an ongoing program at west  liberty and i'm hoping to join in more  with that in the future and actually  training one-on-one students over the  summer to to do bioinformatic research  and then really specific to  bioinformatics we offer the  bioinformatics boot camp every summer  i say every summer last year was our  first year offering it and this year  will be our second  but this is a three-week event um  it requires no previous experience we go  from  having no computational experience to  doing basic bioinformatic pipelines by  the end of this project  so  we start with an introduction to linux  and the command line  then we go in the next couple of weeks  into specific pipelines so we give them  a script that has the pipeline and then  we break this pipeline down into  digestible chunks for them so that by  the end of the week they have run the  entire thing and they can reproduce  those results if they wanted to  so we do this for a coven 19 variant  analysis and a mouse rna-seq analysis  and the data that we use is actually  from  the genomics core it's been generated by  inbree researchers in west virginia  here's kind of an example of um how the  lecture might go we do a little  powerpoint introduction typically  and at the end of it we may have some  step-by-step directions for them to  follow and explore and learn things  about the the input files and the  analyses that they're going to run  so it's  very one-on-one very guided  research they work in pairs typically  that just kind of happened naturally in  this first boot camp people sitting next  to each other kind of helping each other  out and then  jim and i would kind of circle around  and and come in when we needed to  to help them troubleshoot and get  through whatever problems they are  having  so i think this is the most important  aspect rather than the content of what  we're teaching that's pretty commonplace  and i think pretty standardized in a lot  of ways  but uh  i think the interaction that we're  having the one-on-one the guided  help that they're getting is really  important to these students  so here's an example of um  a path that we  might follow  a pipeline that we might provide them so  this is our  variant analysis on the sars coven 19  data  so  we would show them this at the beginning  and they kind of lose their minds  because it looks like gibberish and then  we walk through step by step you know  what is each of each of these commands  doing  what does this mean what happens if you  modify it in this way or that way  and then they get to see  from start to finish how this pipeline  will get executed  so we go from the raw data all the way  through to calling variants and then  looking at them  comparing them across  different samples assigned to different  students and they can compare their  variants to one another  so this is currently just open to west  virginia embry undergraduates  so that's anybody enrolled currently in  a west virginia network institution  um so it is kind of limited in terms of  who can participate at this point um and  we are capping it at about 10 students  for now  this is still kind of in the pilot phase  i would say um we're still developing  the content and the kind of core  material uh for this boot camp but we  plan on continuing doing this for  several years to come  the students at the end of this get to  do a poster presentation on um you know  an insilico manipulation of one of the  pipelines  they get to kind of test out manipulate  some of the steps use a different  program see what the output looks like  change the parameters of a particular  program  and see how that affects their  downstream results and so that's what  they present together  at this  poster  presentation at the west virginia summer  symposium  so that will happen this year as well  it'll be at west virginia university  this year  so we have a lot of potential i think to  expand participation participation in  this event  again once we kind of have our core  material down our method figured out  we will hopefully be able to share  and publish some of these curricular  modules  so we are planning on doing this but  again this is just kind of the initial  phases a really exciting time to be a  part of the development of this  bioinformatic expertise  so  if anyone is on here that is a member of  west virginia inbury  tell all of your students have them  apply  they can find us online  you can email me about it directly if  you'd like  it's a pretty good deal they get a  stipend they get housing and food paid  for  and they get to learn all of these  bioinformatic topics  and with that i think i will take  any questions that anyone has  great thank you very much nicole that  was an amazing presentation  we have questions for you okay great the  first one  yeah the first one is what is special  about people who return back to  education later in life  in terms of their ability to learn  bioinformatics and especially what  career options they end up choosing  so i'm not sure about the career options  part of that but i will say that the  non-traditional students that i've  worked with to date  and again it's a small sample size it's  a small school but they tend to be much  more focused they have a much greater  appreciation  of  you know what bioinformatics is good for  what it could be used for kind of more  perspective i guess is what they come  back with  so they are more open to kind of  exploring these methods and there seem  to be more interested than a lot of the  really young students  that come you know straight from high  school  in the career opportunities especially  so you know when i say that you can work  you know for a hospital you could work  for a private company you work for  the federal fish and wildlife service  there are all kinds of different  positions opening up for people with  these types of skill sets  so i think they see the value in that  yes definitely  so along similar lines to that um what  is the minimal set of skills  a biology oriented student must have  to kind of begin their journey into  bioinformatics  so i would say that  i kind of hesitate to frame it as the  minimal number of skills just because i  think that people with no experience are  more than capable  of picking up these skills very quickly  and i've seen it happen  i think what's more important is not  having um the fear that's associated  with kind of the stigma of oh i'm not  good at computers or i'm not good at  math um and they think that they need to  be  um like i said like rockstar geniuses  when it comes to all of these different  fields when really what's most important  is they love biology and they're willing  to  learn and try um to learn the computer  part of it  and so students who really don't like to  fail  seem to have a lot of trouble with  bioinformatics because it's  mostly failure um you know you try  something doesn't work you troubleshoot  and you figure out the problem so it is  a it's definitely a skill and a muscle  that not all of them have developed  but i think it's definitely worthwhile  even for students who don't want to  pursue a career in bioinformatics just  for the problem-solving skills alone i  think it's very valuable  yes definitely  another question does such a small  undergraduate population have benefits  when it comes to bioinformatics research  i think it does and it's kind of the  same  same set of benefits that really  are in our favor when it comes to  research we get that one-on-one approach  when the students have a question  they just walk right into my office and  talk to me about it  so they have a lot of access to  expertise  that i think you might not experience at  a larger school where you're kind of  just one of many  students in a course  and you know when i teach a  bioinformatics course i have less than  10 people typically especially at these  early stages of developing the  coursework  so i get to work with them directly and  really adapt my teaching style  and the content of the course to them  and their interests  definitely  so another question which of the  programs that you've mentioned  seemed to the greatest learning impact  on young students  of the different courses  yes i think that's what was okay  um  i think the the  the biological programming  um has the potential to have a big  impact  uh because it teaches these basic  programming skills i think it's like a  foot in the door into computer science  that they may not have experienced  before  so they know pretty quickly i think  whether or not  they like it  and then that's kind of like their first  taste that brings them  further along this career path toward  becoming a bioinformatician  great thank you and then one final  question have you had any of the your  boot camp projects results in  application  not yet  um but that would be fantastic if we  could get there one day it's such a kind  of  short program in the grand scheme of  things  we do have like the sure program is like  a nine week program and we have had  student publications result from that  so i think  it might need a little bit more time to  develop than just the three weeks but if  we had a student that was interested  they could always pick up with student  research at their institution  um or collaborate with west liberty  um into the future so  there's the potential there but it  hasn't happened yet  definitely thank you  okay  i think that is all thank you again very  much for coming and speaking with us  today dr garrison thank you
 

